| `io` | `MmioRegion` (8/16/32/64-bit), `Port<T>`, `read_msr`/`write_msr` |
| `msix` | `MsixTable`, `MsixInterrupt::register_for(table, index, &'static T, handler)` (16 slots) |
| `interrupt` | `LegacyInterrupt::register_level_for(dev, &'static T, handler)` / `register_irq_for(irq, …)` (INTx, 8 slots) |
| `sync` | **Locks own what they guard**: `Mutex<T>` (sleeps), `SpinLock<T>` (the kernel's), `IrqSpinLock<T>` and `PreemptSpinLock<T>` (const-constructible, for statics; the first also `try_lock`), `TryLock<T>` (never waited for; the holder may sleep). `lock()` gives a guard that derefs to the data. `WaitGroup`, `Event` (one waiter blocks, anyone signals — hard IRQ included; a blocked waiter's CPU gets an IPI) |
| `once` | `Once<T>` (set once by boot or a `setup`, read from anywhere) and `OnceBox<T>` (the one of something, made on first use; racing makers get the same one) |
| `percpu` | `PerCpu<T>` + `LocalCounter` (statistics any CPU may read, added to with no bus lock), `CpuLocal<T>` (state only its own CPU touches, reached with interrupts off through `with`) |
| `static_ring` | `StaticRing<N>` (MPMC queue of words in static storage) and `Mailbox<T, N>` (fixed-shape messages written and read in place, from any context, no allocation) |
| `task` | `spawn`, `spawn_on`, `spawn_for(name, &'static T, fn(&'static T))` (a service's task over its one instance — no raw context), `spawn_with_ctx` (each takes the name `ps` and `top` show), `TaskHandle` (`id`), `sleep`, `yield_to_runnable`, `current_id` (is this call from one of my own tasks?) |
| `softirq` | `raise`, `register_for(type, &'static T, handler)` (types: `TYPE_NET_RX`, `TYPE_BLK_IO`, etc.) |
| `timer` | `Timer::start_for(period, &'static T, handler)` (periodic, 8 slots, fires on CPU 0 via IPI) |
| `cpu` | `id`, `count`, `online_mask`, `run_on` (IPI), `synchronize` (returns once every interrupt handler running at the call has returned) |
| `ring` | `LocklessRing` — the kernel's bounded MPMC queue of words, one CAS an operation, safe from any context |
| `block` | `trait BlockDriver` + `register_driver(name, parent, &'static D)` → `BlockDeviceRegistration` (`const ASYNC` with `submit`/`kick`: the asynchronous path); `count`/`at` walk the kernel's table, `Disk::name`/`parent`/`handle` say what a device is; `Disk` — an existing disk or partition by name: synchronous `read`/`write`/`flush`, `partitions`; asynchronous `submit(&BlockIo, kick)`/`kick` straight to physical memory, completion callback from interrupt context (`can_submit`: NVMe and its partitions); `claim` → `DiskClaim` before writing (refused while a mount, the disk log or another writer holds it or an overlapping disk/partition) |
| `cmd` | `Command::register` — a shell command whose handler writes to an `Output`; unregistered on drop, after any running call returns. `dispatch` — run a command line as the console would, its output handed to a closure as it is printed |
| `net` | `trait NetDriver` (`type Tx`, `type Rx`) + `NetBinding::new(driver, tx, rx).register(name, mac)` → `NetDeviceHandle`; `TxQueue` (`dequeue`, `done`) and `RxQueue` (`enqueue`, `deliver(&mut FrameBatch<N>)`) exist only inside `flush_tx` / `process_rx`; `NetFrame` (`alloc_tx`, `alloc_rx`); `Nic` — an existing device by name: `ip`, `mac`, `listen(port, &'static impl UdpHandler)` → `UdpListener` (the handler is lent each frame as a `Lent`: `bytes()`, or `retain()` to keep it and answer in it; `listen_batched` adds `on_batch_end`), `RxOwned<T>` (state only the receive path touches, reached with the `&mut RxContext` a handler is lent), `TxBatch<N>` (replies gathered and sent with one lock and one doorbell), `AtomicNic`, `transmit`; the raw `listen_udp`/`transmit_raw` remain for a module whose context is not `'static` |
| `tcp` | `TcpSocket` — a connection somebody else opened and closes (the TLS client's); `TcpListener::bind(&Nic, port)` → `accept(timeout)` → `TcpStream` (`send_all`, `send` with a timeout, `recv`, `peer`), each closed on drop — a listener's drop resets what it never accepted |
| `entropy` | `trait Source` + `register_source(name, &'static S)` — a hardware generator the pool reseeds from |
| `fs` | `read(path, max)`, `write(path, data)` (replaces, then syncs), `create_dir` — a module's configuration files |
| `pod` | `unsafe trait Pod` — said once of a `#[repr(C)]` structure of integers with no padding — and then `pod::read`/`write`/`zeroed`: bounds-checked, unaligned, safe to call. How an on-disk or on-wire structure is read out of a buffer |

## Where `unsafe` goes, and where it does not

The layers (`block/`, `fs/`, `net/`) are ordinary Rust; `unsafe` belongs in
`kcore`, under a type that says why it is sound, and at the C ABI.

- **A lock owns its data.** Never a bare lock beside an `UnsafeCell` with
  `unsafe { &mut *cell.get() }` at every use. A lock that guards nothing is
  `Lock<()>`. Guards are values: hold two, drop them in either order, return
  one from a function (`Tcp::take_fresh`).
- **What waits when dropped** — a `UdpListener` (for the receive path), a
  `TaskHandle` (for the task) — sits in its own `Lock<Option<..>>`: `take()`
  it under the lock, drop it after. Dropped under the lock its wait needs,
  it is a deadlock.
- **The one of something** is a `Once`/`OnceBox` static, not an `AtomicPtr`
  and a `&*ptr`.
- **Per-CPU state** is `PerCpu<LocalCounter…>` for numbers and `CpuLocal<T>`
  for anything exclusive — not an `UnsafeCell<[T; MAX_CPUS]>` indexed by
  `cpu::id()`, which is a data race the moment a task migrates between the
  two.
- **A handle from outside is looked up, not dereferenced**: a device, a TCP
  connection and a block device are each a slot of a table that lives as
  long as the kernel (`DEVICES.by_handle`, `TCP.by_handle`, block
  `device()`), so a word that is not one is `None` rather than undefined
  behaviour — and the functions taking them need not be `unsafe`.
- **Seams inside Rust are Rust**: a trait or a `&mut dyn`, not an
  `extern "C" fn(ctx: *mut u8, …)` table (`vfs::FileSystem`, `http::Sink`,
  `UdpHandler`; a partition is an entry of the block table, not a driver
  registered with it). The C ABI is for what C++ or a separately linked
  module is on the other side of.
- **A driver is a trait impl over a device that lives for good.** The
  kernel's callback tables are C, but no driver sees them: `kcore` has the
  `extern "C"` trampolines, generic over the driver, and the driver
  implements `BlockDriver`, `NetDriver` or `entropy::Source`. A device is
  `Box::leak`ed -- the tables never give one back -- so it is `&'static`,
  there is no `*mut Device` to cast from `ctx`, and shutting down is a
  `quiesce(&self)` that stops the hardware and takes the interrupt handles
  out of their lock, not a `Box::from_raw` and a `Drop`.
- **A callback is a function item over its target**:
  `register_for(.., target: &'static T, handler)`, where `handler` is
  `fn(&'static T)` named directly (`Nic::interrupt`, not a closure that
  captures). `kcore` checks at compile time that it is zero-sized and
  monomorphizes the trampoline, so the context word *is* the target.
- **Who may touch what is who is handed it.** An interrupt handler gets
  `&'static Device` and nothing else: registers, atomics, IRQ-safe locks --
  it can run beside itself when its vector moves. A NIC's rings are not in
  the device at all: they are `NetDriver::Tx` / `Rx`, owned by the
  `NetBinding`, lent as `&mut` to `flush_tx` (the transmit lock is held)
  and to `process_rx` (the receive soft IRQ runs on one CPU at a time).
  What a state dump needs of a ring the poll publishes in atomics, and the
  descriptors themselves are shared cells it can read live.
- **Device memory is cells, not `read_volatile` on a cast pointer**: a
  descriptor is a `#[repr(C)]` struct of `Volatile<u32>` (`unsafe impl
  Descriptor` says so once), a ring is `leak_ring`'s `&'static [D]`, and
  anything else in a `DmaBuffer` crosses through `load`/`store`/`bytes`.
  The barriers stay where they were: `dma_wmb` before the store that hands
  ownership over, `dma_rmb` after the check that takes it back.
- **A graph is an arena and ids**, not raw pointers: `fs::vnode::Tree` hands
  out `NodeId`s -- a slot and a generation -- so a stale one finds nothing.
  Open files are the same shape (`vfs::Handle`).
- **An export that trusts a pointer is `unsafe extern "C" fn`** with a
  `# Safety` section; one that takes only validated handles and plain values
  is a safe `extern "C" fn`.
- **An export nobody calls is deleted**, not kept for symmetry.

## Interrupt handlers

- **MSI-X**: `MsixInterrupt::register_for(&table, index, dev, Dev::interrupt)` — no assembly needed, 16 slots shared across all Rust drivers.
- **Legacy INTx**: `LegacyInterrupt::register_level_for(pci_dev, dev, Dev::interrupt)`, 8 slots. The line may be shared: the handler says by its own status register whether the interrupt was its.
- `dev` is `&'static Dev`, the handler `fn(&'static Dev)` — called from IRQ context: no sleeping, no allocation, and nothing of the device but registers, atomics and IRQ-safe locks (it may run on two CPUs at once).
- The returned handle unregisters on drop. Keep it in a lock inside the device (`IrqSpinLock<Option<Irqs>>`); `quiesce` takes it out and drops it after the lock.

## Block driver registration

```rust
impl kcore::block::BlockDriver for MyDisk {
    const ASYNC: bool = false;            // true: also `submit` and `kick`
    fn capacity(&self) -> u64 { self.sectors }
    fn sector_size(&self) -> u64 { 512 }
    fn read(&'static self, sector: u64, buf: &mut [u8]) -> bool { ... }
    fn write(&'static self, sector: u64, data: &[u8], fua: bool) -> bool { ... }
    fn flush(&'static self) -> bool { ... }   // default: nothing to push
}

let disk: &'static MyDisk = Box::leak(Box::new(MyDisk { ... }));
let reg = kcore::block::register_driver("nvme0", 0, disk)?;
// reg lives forever (boot-lifetime); no unregister
```

Calls arrive from any task on any CPU, several at once: what one needs
exclusively sits behind a lock in the device (`SpinLock<Submission>`). A
buffer is whole sectors and never empty; the device may DMA straight into it.

## Net driver registration

```rust
impl kcore::net::NetDriver for MyNic {
    type Tx = TxRing;   // only flush_tx touches it
    type Rx = RxRing;   // only process_rx touches it

    fn flush_tx(&'static self, ring: &mut TxRing, stack: &mut TxQueue<'_>) {
        ring.reap_completed(stack);              // finished frames -> stack.done(frame)
        while ring.has_space() {
            let Some(frame) = stack.dequeue() else { break };
            ring.submit(frame);                  // the ring keeps the frame until HW is done
        }
        // doorbell
    }

    fn process_rx(&'static self, ring: &mut RxRing, up: &mut RxQueue<'_>) {
        let mut batch: FrameBatch<64> = FrameBatch::new();
        while let Some(frame) = ring.harvest() { batch.push(frame); /* repost a fresh one */ }
        up.deliver(&mut batch);                  // one lock for the lot
    }
}

let binding = NetBinding::new(MyNic { regs, .. }, tx_ring, rx_ring);   // leaked, for good
let nic = binding.driver();                     // &'static MyNic: point the interrupt at it
// ... register the interrupt, enable the hardware ...
let handle = binding.register("eth0", mac)?;    // last: from here on it is called
handle.set_ip(0xC0A80001); // 192.168.0.1 host order
```

**TX frame lifetime**: a `NetFrame` from `dequeue()` wraps the DMA buffer. Keep it (in the ring's `Vec<Option<NetFrame>>` shadow) until the hardware says it is done, then hand it to `stack.done(frame)` — never drop it under the transmit lock: a free can reach the page allocator's TLB shootdown, which a CPU spinning on that lock never answers.

**RX frame**: `NetFrame::alloc_rx(max_size)` gives a frame with `len() == 0`. Use `data_phys()` for the HW ring entry. After HW fills the buffer, call `set_len(received)` then `up.enqueue(frame)` or gather a `FrameBatch` for `up.deliver`.

## C++ FFI conventions (`kernel/rust_ffi.cpp`)

- All FFI implementation lives in `src/cpp/kernel/rust_ffi.cpp`.
- Register/create functions: heap-allocate adapters with `Mm::TAlloc<T, RustAllocTag>()` — never use static arrays of non-trivial C++ objects (static constructors are unreliable in freestanding).
- Slot pools for IRQ/timer/MSI-X callbacks: protect with `RawRwSpinLock` — read-lock in handlers, write-lock+IRQ-save for registration/deregistration.
- Block/net device adapters: no slot lock needed — registration is boot-time only, no unregister.
- Handles are raw pointers cast to `unsigned long`; 0 = failure.
